        "scenario": "A manufacturer sues a logistics provider over an interrupted delivery contract; an employee preserved some messages but deleted others under a routine schedule.",
        "reading": "Begin by identifying who can sue whom and in which forum. Distinguish the legally sufficient allegations needed for a complaint from facts that ultimately must be proved. Create an allegation-to-element matrix separating contractual duty, performance, breach, causation, damages and potential defenses. For any early motion, explain its procedural standard rather than treating it as an immediate fact trial. A litigation hold should identify custodians, systems and foreseeable evidence reasonably before materials disappear; evaluate preservation under applicable circumstances without assuming deletion proves bad faith. A proportionate discovery plan identifies the disputed claim elements, documents likely to address them, privacy burdens, time limits and protective measures. Under Rule 26(b)(1), relevance and proportionality must be argued with particular facts. If electronic evidence was lost, Rule 37(e) analysis is distinct from mere nonproduction. Work both sides: claimant's document requests and defendant's objections. Consider venue, personal jurisdiction, removal and arbitration as open questions to research, not assumed facts. Draft a concise proposed case-management order that could be discussed with an actual court, subject to local rules. This is instructional draft content; verify current controlling legal authority and jurisdiction before relying on it.\n\nTARGETED PRIMARY-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Rule 37(e) applies to electronically stored information that should have been preserved, was lost because reasonable steps were not taken, and cannot be restored or replaced. The prejudice-based curative measures in (e)(1) differ from the serious adverse-inference or terminating measures in (e)(2), which require intent to deprive. Routine deletion alone does not prove that intent. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_37. Verification scope: Rule 37(e) text and committee note; 2015 revision. This is a bounded opinion/rule-text check; later binding treatment and local variations still require review.",
