        "reading": "Distinguish a genuine dispute over a material fact from an unsupported accusation. Identify governing contractual elements and the party bearing trial proof. Evaluate the declarations for personal knowledge, admissible form and contradictions. Map each claim element to deposition testimony, the GPS source and outstanding discovery. A partial record can support a Rule 56(d) request if specified needed discovery could affect the decision.\n\nPRACTICAL REASONING WORKFLOW: Identify the exact governing forum, time period and parties before applying any rule. Prepare a factual chronology and separate facts supported by documents or testimony from allegations, inferences and information not yet available. State the rule in your own words and verify its current primary-law text; a textbook summary is not itself binding authority. Apply the competing arguments above to each necessary legal element and explain what evidence would change the conclusion. In the proposed ruling or advice, address procedure, remedy, costs of uncertainty and professional responsibilities. For self-assessment, compare your first conclusion with an alternative in which one pivotal fact changes and explain precisely which element changes. Retain the written product as a portfolio draft for qualified examiner review.\n\nTARGETED PRIMARY-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 56(a), summary judgment requires no genuine dispute as to any material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law. Rule 56(c) addresses record support; Rule 56(d) provides a procedure when the nonmovant cannot yet present essential facts for specified reasons. Do not treat unsupported lawyer statements as competing evidence. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_56. Verification scope: Rule 56(a), (c), (d). This is a bounded opinion/rule-text check; later binding treatment and local variations still require review.",
