        "scenario": "A defective-valve trial involves a repaired component, an engineer's reconstruction, and a witness who heard an account from a shift manager.",
        "reading": "For every proposed exhibit, first identify the inference that counsel wants the jury to draw and the link in the chain of proof. Authentication establishes that an item is what its proponent claims, but does not automatically resolve hearsay, relevance or reliability. The repaired component may require chain-of-custody testimony and a documented comparison to its earlier state. Distinguish a witness's firsthand observations from a secondhand narrative about the accident; analyze each statement separately for hearsay and any exception. For the engineer, determine qualifications, sufficient data, reliable principles and reliable application under current Rule 702, rather than assuming credentials settle admissibility. Rule 403 objections must identify a genuine risk of unfair prejudice, confusion or waste rather than general disagreement. Build proposed jury instructions from actual elements and burdens and preserve objections under Rule 51. Do not tell a jury that a lawyer's argument is evidence. If an exhibit is excluded, consider an accurate offer of proof and the standard for appellate review. Compare a full admission, a limited-purpose admission and exclusion on a disputed foundation.\n\nAPPLIED PRACTICE AND RESEARCH: Establish the controlling jurisdiction, procedural setting and date before making a legal claim. Create an evidence-to-element matrix distinguishing established facts, allegations, missing records and competing inferences. Cite the exact language of the relevant rule, statute or court decision rather than relying on a secondary summary. Assess how a different material fact would change the likely remedy or professional recommendation. Draft both sides fairly and record unresolved primary-source and later-treatment questions for review by a qualified legal examiner. This is a fictional educational scenario and does not establish personal legal advice or independent competency.\n\nPRIMARY-RULE VERIFICATION (November 2026 research checkpoint): Federal Rule of Evidence 702, amended in December 2023, requires the proponent to establish that the expert-admissibility conditions are more likely than not satisfied under Rule 104(a); do not consign sufficiency of basis and reliable application exclusively to the jury. Primary rule source: https://www.uscourts.gov/forms-rules/current-rules-practice-procedure/federal-rules-evidence ; amended-rule text and committee explanation: https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_702 . Confirm the currently effective text again at examination time.",
