        "reading": "Begin with a realistic damages model rather than attaching a single probability to winning. Separate gross receipts, costs avoided, incremental contribution margin, mitigation expenses and contract limitation clauses. Document which components could be proved by competent evidence. Explore liability risk and collection risk independently: a favorable verdict is not the same as receiving payment. Identify who has actual settlement authority and whether a client needs insurer or board approval. Rule 68 has particular scope and cost consequences; do not assume it applies identically to a defendant's offer or to all fee-shifting claims. Prepare a confidentiality and enforcement analysis for any proposed terms, including release scope, payment schedule, default, retained jurisdiction and appropriate dismissal language. A judge or mediator may facilitate discussion but does not replace the client's informed choice. Model how a newly discovered substitute supplier affects mitigation and the recommended range. Use ranges with transparent assumptions, not fabricated certainty.\n\nAPPLIED PRACTICE AND RESEARCH: Establish the controlling jurisdiction, procedural setting and date before making a legal claim. Create an evidence-to-element matrix distinguishing established facts, allegations, missing records and competing inferences. Cite the exact language of the relevant rule, statute or court decision rather than relying on a secondary summary. Assess how a different material fact would change the likely remedy or professional recommendation. Draft both sides fairly and record unresolved primary-source and later-treatment questions for review by a qualified legal examiner. This is a fictional educational scenario and does not establish personal legal advice or independent competency.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Rule 68(a) permits a party defending against a claim to make a qualifying offer of judgment at least 14 days before the date set for trial; acceptance and cost-shifting provisions have their own conditions. This is not a generic rule governing all informal settlement offers. The lesson's litigation risk worksheet should separately distinguish ordinary negotiation, Rule 68 offers, and fee issues governed by other law. Primary text/reference: https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_68. This checks the specified proposition only; subsequent treatment, local law and application to a real record still require further review.",
