        "title": "Preserving appellate issues through trial",
        "authority": "Federal Rules of Civil Procedure 46, 50, 51, 59 and 61; Federal Rules of Evidence 103 and 702",
        "scenario": "In a complex products trial, counsel disputes a demonstrative chart, a disputed expert conclusion, and a proposed jury instruction. The judge rules orally but the record is ambiguous.",
        "reading": "An appellate preservation plan begins during trial rather than after an adverse verdict. Identify the exact item, ground, ruling, party harmed, and relevant record citation. Under Evidence Rule 103, analyze whether an objection or offer of proof sufficiently informed the court of the issue and whether the ruling was definitive; do not assume a repeated objection is always necessary or never necessary. Under Civil Rule 51, distinguish an instruction request, objection, and plain-error issues. Rule 50 presents a distinct pathway to challenge evidentiary sufficiency; identify when the required motion is made and renewed. A Rule 59 motion for new trial asks different questions from a judgment-as-a-matter-of-law motion. Describe which procedural options may depend on local rules and current appellate authority. Construct a preservation matrix with alternative rulings: if the expert method is excluded, what proof remains; if the instruction is refused, how does that affect the jury's path to liability? Develop an accurate, contemporaneous trial record without asserting that every alleged error mandates reversal.\n\nPRACTICE METHOD — This is an educational hypothetical, not advice for a real client. Create a chronology and an evidence matrix before reaching a conclusion. Identify legal elements and burdens, jurisdiction, operative date, unresolved facts and source hierarchy. Research any changed statute or precedent and record the date checked. Distinguish what a party alleged from what testimony or documents actually establish. Present a reasonable adverse argument and identify how at least one different fact would affect the legal conclusion or remedy. Prepare your written deliverable for a qualified human examiner rather than treating a successful quiz response as mastery.\n\nPRIMARY-RULE VERIFICATION (November 2026 research checkpoint): Rule 50(a) concerns judgment as a matter of law after a party has been fully heard at jury trial and requires stating the grounds before submission to the jury; Rule 50(b) is a *renewal* after trial and the rule text states a 28-day postjudgment or postdischarge period in the specified circumstances. An ordinary new-trial motion under Rule 59 has a different role. Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_50 . Verify actual judgment date, tolling rules and local procedure; do not describe Rule 50(b) as an entirely new unpreserved motion.",
