        "scenario": "A court's pretrial order omits the plaintiff's alternative damages theory; counsel discovers the omission four days before trial.",
        "reading": "A final pretrial order shapes the issues and evidence that will be tried. Identify exactly which theory was pleaded, disclosed, preserved and omitted. Do not assume a late request is automatically granted or that any omission irrevocably waives the argument. Analyze the Rule 16(e) standard and material prejudice, the reasons for omission, available continuance, witness disclosure, and the opposing party's preparation. Compare exclusion to narrower corrective measures. Develop a date-by-date chronology linking counsel's actions with the local schedule. Draft a motion that candidly addresses fault and the adverse party's strongest prejudice argument. Separate the legal standard for modifying a final pretrial order from the standard for admitting a specific late-disclosed exhibit.\n\nDETAILED PRACTICE PROTOCOL: Establish the parties, governing jurisdiction, procedural posture and applicable date before choosing the rule. Build a fact-to-element table with columns for supporting evidence, adverse evidence, unknown inputs, burden and possible remedy. State the best legally grounded position for each party, then identify two fact changes that might alter the answer. Use authentic primary sources and check for later changes or controlling local authority. Written submissions must distinguish a sourced legal proposition from a plausible inference and explicitly disclose uncertainty. A qualified human examiner must review the result; a knowledge check is formative feedback, not a professional grade.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Civil Rule 16(e) specifically provides that a final pretrial order may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Do not substitute the different Rule 16(b)(4) good-cause standard for changes to a scheduling order. The lesson should evaluate surprise, diligence, prejudice and feasible cure under the correct order type and controlling cases. Primary text/reference: https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_16. This checks the specified proposition only; subsequent treatment, local law and application to a real record still require further review.",
