        "reading": "Separate the role of prosecutor and defense counsel before analyzing the facts. The prosecutor must evaluate the evidence, charging elements, exculpatory and impeachment information and disclosure deadlines under controlling law; the defense should test legality of the arrest, any search, identification reliability, witness access, and available pretrial motions. Neither side can ethically assume the alarm proves guilt. Record exactly what the video shows, who controls it, whether it was reviewed and what material remains unknown. Brady/Giglio obligations require attention to favorable material and impeachment evidence, but rules of timing and remedies depend on procedural posture and applicable jurisdiction. Investigate whether the arrest, post-arrest statement and identification are distinct events raising different constitutional claims. Develop a preservation and disclosure letter that does not assert nonexistent facts. Build a plea discussion worksheet that distinguishes factual basis, consequences, mitigation, immigration or collateral consequences that require specialized review, and the client's autonomous decision. The aim is to practice professional judgment, not to simulate legal advice for a real prosecution. This is instructional draft content; verify current controlling legal authority and jurisdiction before relying on it.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Brady v. Maryland, 373 U.S. 83 (1963), concerns prosecution suppression of favorable material evidence relevant to guilt or punishment. Do not infer that the mere possibility of contradictory footage automatically establishes a constitutional violation or guarantees dismissal; the evidence, its favorability, withholding and materiality must be examined, alongside any broader jurisdiction-specific disclosure obligations.\nReviewed text: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/373/83. This is a bounded check of specified provisions, not an exhaustive case-law, citator, later-treatment or state-adoption review.",
