        "authority": "28 U.S.C. §2254; applicable exhaustion, limitation and procedural-default law",
        "scenario": "A state prisoner asks counsel to file a federal habeas petition while a state postconviction appeal remains pending.",
        "reading": "Federal habeas review of state convictions is not another ordinary trial. Identify the judgment, direct-review and collateral-review timeline, available state remedies and whether claims were fairly presented through the required channels. Separate federal constitutional claims from state-law errors and distinguish exhaustion, procedural default, limitations and merits deference. Calculating a filing deadline requires verified dates and statutory tolling rules; do not assume a pending state application always saves every filing. Identify whether the record supports any recognized exception or need for fact development, and clarify standards that can bar new evidence in federal court. Counsel must give a careful written explanation of litigation risk and preserve the client's remaining options. Construct an alternative strategy for a potentially unexhausted claim without promising automatic stay or relief.\n\nDETAILED PRACTICE PROTOCOL: Establish the parties, governing jurisdiction, procedural posture and applicable date before choosing the rule. Build a fact-to-element table with columns for supporting evidence, adverse evidence, unknown inputs, burden and possible remedy. State the best legally grounded position for each party, then identify two fact changes that might alter the answer. Use authentic primary sources and check for later changes or controlling local authority. Written submissions must distinguish a sourced legal proposition from a plausible inference and explicitly disclose uncertainty. A qualified human examiner must review the result; a knowledge check is formative feedback, not a professional grade.\n\nTARGETED PRIMARY-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): For a state prisoner challenging a judgment in federal court, 28 U.S.C. §2254(b) generally requires exhaustion of available state remedies, subject to statutory exceptions. Separately, 28 U.S.C. §2244(d) defines a one-year limitation period with alternative statutory trigger dates and a rule excluding time while a properly filed relevant state postconviction application is pending. The deadline cannot be calculated without the actual history; tolling questions require current controlling cases. Source: https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?edition=prelim&num=0&req=granuleid%3AUSC-prelim-title28-section2254. Verification scope: 28 U.S.C. §2254(b), plus §2244(d): https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/119-124/t28/s2244/. This is a bounded opinion/rule-text check; later binding treatment and local variations still require review.",
