        "authority": "Fourth, Fifth, Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments; Brady, Giglio, Batson, plea-assistance and postconviction doctrines",
        "scenario": "FICTIONAL THIRD-YEAR FINAL CASE FILE: Investigators rely on a suggestive identification, a disputed residential search and a witness benefit not timely disclosed. A plea offer expires, a sentencing report contains unsupported loss amounts, and new evidence surfaces during state review.",
        "reading": "Construct a criminal-case chronology from investigation to postconviction review. Separate search authorization, identification reliability, disclosure duties, plea advice, sentencing findings and procedural appeal bars. Apply each right at its actual triggering event and specify the burden and relevant remedy. Do not equate nondisclosure with automatic dismissal, a warrant defect with automatic suppression, or poor plea advice with automatic relief. Address prosecution ethics and defense counseling independently and fairly. Build an evidentiary record with contradictions and a list of facts necessary to assess materiality, prejudice and postconviction eligibility. Identify state-specific differences and do not fabricate filing deadlines.\n\nEXAMINATION METHOD: Treat the written answer as a legal research and practice portfolio, not an automatically scored quiz. Define the legal question, exact forum, jurisdiction and operative date. Build an annotated chronology and a fact-to-element matrix, keeping facts supported by the record separate from disputed allegations and unknowns. Verify all primary authorities, actual holdings, current rules and any subsequent treatment; do not invent evidence to fill gaps. Address the strongest adverse legal theory, analyze two significant fact changes, and provide a realistic client or court-facing recommendation. The five rubric dimensions total 100 points. A qualified human examiner must evaluate the substantive written submission and its citations before any mastery determination.\n\nTARGETED PRIMARY-SOURCE RESEARCH NOTE (2026-11-03): The Sixth Amendment assistance-of-counsel inquiry for plea representation requires separating deficient performance from prejudice. A final criminal-case portfolio should identify what counsel advised, what the defendant would have done, whether a plea offer could have been accepted and whether an outcome would have been different, not use an undifferentiated claim of unfairness. Reference: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/566/156/. This is a selected rule, statute, model rule or precedent crosscheck; it is not a complete legal accuracy review, proof of applicability to each jurisdiction, or a subsequent-treatment verification.",
        "counterarguments": "Prosecutor argues good-faith investigation and limited prejudice; defense seeks suppression, disclosure relief, plea remedy or postconviction consideration.",
        "assignment": "FINAL EXAMINATION — Write a 2,000–2,500-word prosecution/defense case memorandum, suppression hearing outline, disclosure log, plea worksheet, sentencing objection and postconviction path table. Submit all referenced materials, a primary-source verification log, two competing positions and a reflection on unresolved issues. This examination remains ungraded until qualified human substantive review; a quiz response alone does not establish mastery.",
