        "id": "L4-3-1",
        "title": "Due diligence, representations and closing conditions",
        "authority": "Delaware General Corporation Law where applicable; relevant LLC law; contract assignment and consent provisions; antitrust filing requirements when triggered",
        "scenario": "A regional food distributor plans to acquire a competing business. During diligence, the buyer discovers that a major customer agreement may terminate upon a change of control.",
        "reading": "Before drafting an acquisition agreement, specify whether the deal is an asset sale, equity sale or merger and which entity laws govern the target and buyer. Each structure changes which rights and liabilities transfer, the necessary corporate approvals, tax questions and third-party consents. Build a diligence schedule including contracts, labor, data security, litigation, indebtedness, intellectual property and regulatory permits. For the customer agreement, read the exact assignment and change-of-control clauses rather than concluding that consent is automatically required in every transaction. Separate a representation about contract validity from a pre-closing covenant to secure consent and from a condition that allows the buyer not to close. Compare indemnity, price adjustment, escrow and termination rights as risk-allocation techniques. Prepare a board memo showing the commercial upside, the possible loss of the major customer and a negotiation alternative. Explain which factual or legal issues require qualified tax, antitrust, employment or specialized counsel; do not claim a standard form resolves them all. This is instructional draft content; verify current controlling legal authority and jurisdiction before relying on it.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): An M&A closing conditions checklist should distinguish corporate and third-party contract consents from the separate premerger filing analysis. The FTC's HSR program covers only certain qualifying mergers and acquisitions; filing obligations and current adjusted thresholds must be screened transaction-by-transaction, and covered parties ordinarily may not close until the applicable waiting period ends or is terminated according to law. A private customer change-of-control clause is not itself an HSR filing requirement. Source: https://www.ftc.gov/enforcement/premerger-notification-program ; https://www.ftc.gov/enforcement/premerger-notification-program/hsr-resources/steps-determining-whether-hsr-filing. This is a limited current FTC regulatory guidance check, not a comprehensive antitrust or transaction legal review.",
        "counterarguments": "Develop the best plausible opposing theory without inventing facts; identify what evidence, statute or procedural posture would change the answer.",
        "assignment": "Draft a 1,400-word acquisition risk memo, a diligence question list, a consent covenant, a tailored representation and a redlined closing condition. Analyze a variation in which the contract prohibits assignment but contains no change-of-control language.",
        "question": "What must be checked before deciding whether customer consent is required for an acquisition?",
        "options": [
          "Assume every change of control automatically breaches all contracts",
          "Read the actual contract and the proposed deal structure under governing law",
          "Assume consent is never needed for equity acquisitions"
        ],
