        "id": "L4-3-3",
        "title": "Regulatory approvals and closing architecture",
        "authority": "Hart-Scott-Rodino Act where thresholds apply; Sherman Act; Clayton Act; transaction-specific regulatory rules",
        "scenario": "Two distributors propose a merger; signing occurs before antitrust clearance and executives want to integrate pricing immediately.",
        "reading": "Begin with an acquisition structure and threshold screen rather than assuming every deal is reportable. Distinguish signing from closing and regulatory waiting periods from business integration. Preclosing coordination over competitively sensitive prices creates potential antitrust exposure. Explain clean-team controls, permissible planning and forbidden premature operational control in general terms, with specialist review for a real transaction. Map conditions, outside date, termination, divestiture risk and allocation of efforts to secure approvals.\n\nPRACTICAL REASONING WORKFLOW: Identify the exact governing forum, time period and parties before applying any rule. Prepare a factual chronology and separate facts supported by documents or testimony from allegations, inferences and information not yet available. State the rule in your own words and verify its current primary-law text; a textbook summary is not itself binding authority. Apply the competing arguments above to each necessary legal element and explain what evidence would change the conclusion. In the proposed ruling or advice, address procedure, remedy, costs of uncertainty and professional responsibilities. For self-assessment, compare your first conclusion with an alternative in which one pivotal fact changes and explain precisely which element changes. Retain the written product as a portfolio draft for qualified examiner review.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): The Hart–Scott–Rodino Act requires qualifying transactions to complete notification and the applicable waiting period before closing; the test depends on adjusted thresholds, entity structure and exemptions, not simply the announced deal value. CURRENT PROCEDURAL NOTE: The FTC reported that a February 12, 2026 district-court judgment vacated the revised HSR form used from February 2025; after denial of a stay on March 19, 2026, filings again use the earlier form and instructions, while the agencies also voluntarily accept filings on the revised materials. Do not teach one permanent filing form or hard-code dated thresholds. Source: https://www.ftc.gov/enforcement/premerger-notification-program ; https://search.ftc.gov/enforcement/premerger-notification-program/hsr-notification-forms-instructions-guidance. This is a limited current FTC regulatory guidance check, not a comprehensive antitrust or transaction legal review.",
        "counterarguments": "Buyer seeks early integration efficiencies; seller highlights competitive independence pending closing.",
        "assignment": "Develop a regulatory checklist, clean-team protocol, outside-date clause and two closing-timeline contingencies. Include a source-verification table identifying the governing jurisdiction, procedural stage and relevant primary authority; provide the opposing argument, two material factual uncertainties, and a short explanation of what would change your recommended outcome. A qualified examiner must evaluate the writing before mastery is recorded.",
        "question": "What is the safer approach to competitively sensitive pricing before a merger closes?",
