        "scenario": "An officer appeals a denial of qualified immunity, asserting that disputed facts should be viewed in the officer's favor.",
        "reading": "Begin by identifying the government actor, right allegedly violated and conduct relevant to the constitutional claim. Qualified-immunity doctrine and appellate jurisdiction are not interchangeable questions. Interlocutory appeals may address certain legal qualified-immunity questions, but appellate courts ordinarily cannot reweigh a district court's finding that the record contains genuine disputes about what occurred. Separate the plaintiff-favorable version of facts assumed at the summary-judgment stage from the ultimate factfinder's resolution. Identify the applicable law as of the alleged conduct and whether precedent clearly established the particular right in context. Examine whether a categorical legal argument is truly independent of a factual challenge. The appellant needs precise record citations and a statement of the reviewable legal issue; do not fabricate admissions to obtain jurisdiction. Discuss potential remand and how a later trial may change the procedural route.\n\nAPPLIED PRACTICE AND RESEARCH: Establish the controlling jurisdiction, procedural setting and date before making a legal claim. Create an evidence-to-element matrix distinguishing established facts, allegations, missing records and competing inferences. Cite the exact language of the relevant rule, statute or court decision rather than relying on a secondary summary. Assess how a different material fact would change the likely remedy or professional recommendation. Draft both sides fairly and record unresolved primary-source and later-treatment questions for review by a qualified legal examiner. This is a fictional educational scenario and does not establish personal legal advice or independent competency.\n\nTARGETED PRIMARY-SOURCE RESEARCH NOTE (2026-11-03): Johnson v. Jones limits immediate appellate review when the qualified-immunity appellant merely challenges a district court's determination that summary-judgment evidence suffices to create a genuine factual dispute. Distinguish reviewable legal questions on assumed facts from a request for appellate factual redetermination. Reference: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/515/304/. This is a selected rule, statute, model rule or precedent crosscheck; it is not a complete legal accuracy review, proof of applicability to each jurisdiction, or a subsequent-treatment verification.",
