        "reading": "Identify the order, exact effective time, procedural path for relief in the trial court, and appellate court authority. Stay relief is not granted solely because compliance costs money or an appeal is filed. Analyze likelihood of success, irreparable harm absent a stay, injury to other parties and the public interest under the applicable standard. Distinguish keeping the status quo from a mandatory alteration of existing legal duties, and examine how the record supports each asserted harm. Explain why a stay pending appeal and an injunction pending appeal may have distinct procedural framing. Present a verifiable emergency schedule with actual deadlines, service requirements and supporting declarations; do not invent an emergency to avoid ordinary process. Draft a narrowly tailored alternative and address potential bond or conditions if relevant.\n\nDETAILED PRACTICE PROTOCOL: Establish the parties, governing jurisdiction, procedural posture and applicable date before choosing the rule. Build a fact-to-element table with columns for supporting evidence, adverse evidence, unknown inputs, burden and possible remedy. State the best legally grounded position for each party, then identify two fact changes that might alter the answer. Use authentic primary sources and check for later changes or controlling local authority. Written submissions must distinguish a sourced legal proposition from a plausible inference and explicitly disclose uncertainty. A qualified human examiner must review the result; a knowledge check is formative feedback, not a professional grade.\n\nREPORTED-CASE VERIFICATION (November 2026 research checkpoint): Nken v. Holder, 556 U.S. 418 (2009), explains the traditional four stay factors: strong likelihood-of-success showing, irreparable harm, injury to other parties and public interest; a stay is not automatic pending appeal. Importantly Nken concerned a *stay of removal*, and the precise standard and statutory limits must be examined for the actual kind of relief and forum. Opinion: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/556/418/ . Do not collapse a stay and an injunction or assume all cases share identical procedural rules.",
