        "reading": "Distinguish the regulator's actual statutory authority from the operator's operational duties. Start by reading the request: is it an informal information request, lawful administrative subpoena, order or inspection notice? Identify return dates, subject matter, time range, privileged information and opportunities to object or seek modification. Map the enabling statute and agency regulations to each requested category. After Loper Bright, judicial interpretation of statutory meaning does not automatically defer to an agency under Chevron, but technical judgments and valid discretionary delegations still require careful analysis. Independently assess the reliability of reported equipment failures, contractor records, preservation obligations, remediation and employee interview protocols. Establish an investigation plan with a factual timeline, witness list, secure collection procedures and internal escalation. Compare cooperation, a narrowly tailored objection and voluntary corrective action; explain the evidentiary and business costs of each. Produce a transparent written response without false assurances that all risks are eliminated. This is a fictional exercise; real regulatory deadlines need qualified counsel. This is instructional draft content; verify current controlling legal authority and jurisdiction before relying on it.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): The federal Administrative Procedure Act does not by itself give every agency unlimited investigative power; first identify the agency's enabling statute and the legal nature of the demand. For any agency regulatory response also distinguish notice-and-comment procedures under §553 from adjudicatory or subpoena procedures governed by other authority.\nReviewed text: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/553. This is a bounded check of specified provisions, not an exhaustive case-law, citator, later-treatment or state-adoption review.",
        "counterarguments": "Develop the best plausible opposing theory without inventing facts; identify what evidence, statute or procedural posture would change the answer.",
        "assignment": "Create a 1,300-word regulatory response memo, evidence-custodian map, 30-day investigation plan, risk register and draft agency response. Analyze changes in which the subpoena is issued without statutory authority and in which the contractor provides incomplete records.",
        "question": "What is the first legal distinction when responding to an agency document demand?",
