        "reading": "Drafting a comment is not the same as lobbying with a slogan. Read the proposal, identify the agency's statutory basis, docket, comment deadline, requested submissions and the technical report's assumptions. Explain what the data can and cannot support: sample design, comparison group, uncertainty, cost modeling and affected operators. An effective comment provides specific facts and feasible alternatives, recognizes the agency's legitimate safety goals and requests clarifications grounded in the statute. Avoid fabricated data and identify proprietary submissions that may need special handling. Discuss how to preserve substantive objections for potential judicial review and why a failure to engage the record can weaken later challenges under applicable law. Distinguish comments on a proposed rule from petitions for reconsideration and challenges to a final rule. Prepare a proposed regulatory alternative with measurable implementation milestones and a response to the agency's strongest objection.\n\nAPPLIED PRACTICE AND RESEARCH: Establish the controlling jurisdiction, procedural setting and date before making a legal claim. Create an evidence-to-element matrix distinguishing established facts, allegations, missing records and competing inferences. Cite the exact language of the relevant rule, statute or court decision rather than relying on a secondary summary. Assess how a different material fact would change the likely remedy or professional recommendation. Draft both sides fairly and record unresolved primary-source and later-treatment questions for review by a qualified legal examiner. This is a fictional educational scenario and does not establish personal legal advice or independent competency.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Under 5 U.S.C. §553(b)–(c), covered federal notice-and-comment rulemaking ordinarily requires adequate notice and an opportunity for interested persons to submit written data, views or arguments, followed by a concise general statement of basis and purpose. Important statutory exemptions and good-cause exceptions exist, and §553 does not supply every state agency's procedural rules. Identify the actual docket and filing period.\nReviewed text: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/553. This is a bounded check of specified provisions, not an exhaustive case-law, citator, later-treatment or state-adoption review.",
