        "reading": "Identify the type of regulated industry, inspection authority, actual statutory conditions and whether a warrant, lawful exception or valid consent is available. Administrative inspections differ from ordinary criminal searches, and special rules may govern closely regulated industries; do not assume either limitless access or an absolute warrant requirement. Distinguish a routine inspection from an emergency and identify who has authority to consent on behalf of the facility. Record the inspection scope, records requested, permitted observation and personnel interviews without coaching employees to obstruct or destroy material. Explain possible administrative and judicial review mechanisms, sanctions for unlawful refusal where applicable and preservation duties. Draft a respectful response that seeks clarification when scope is uncertain rather than giving an unsupported refusal. Provide alternate analysis if an inspector demands unrelated private personnel data.\n\nDETAILED PRACTICE PROTOCOL: Establish the parties, governing jurisdiction, procedural posture and applicable date before choosing the rule. Build a fact-to-element table with columns for supporting evidence, adverse evidence, unknown inputs, burden and possible remedy. State the best legally grounded position for each party, then identify two fact changes that might alter the answer. Use authentic primary sources and check for later changes or controlling local authority. Written submissions must distinguish a sourced legal proposition from a plausible inference and explicitly disclose uncertainty. A qualified human examiner must review the result; a knowledge check is formative feedback, not a professional grade.\n\nTARGETED SOURCE REVIEW (2026-11-03): See v. City of Seattle, 387 U.S. 541 (1967), recognized a warrant requirement for unconsented routine administrative entry into private commercial premises in its context, while Camara v. Municipal Court addressed housing inspections. Later law recognizes specific administrative-search and closely regulated industry doctrines. Do not teach that every inspector may enter without any lawful basis or that all regulated premises always require the same ordinary criminal warrant. Sources: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/387/541/ ; https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/464/287. Scope: limited identified primary-law or agency-reference check; not a full case citator, state-law verification, individualized legal opinion, or academic sign-off.",
