        "scenario": "FICTIONAL THIRD-YEAR FINAL CASE FILE: A transportation operator faces a subpoena after safety failures. A whistleblower reports altered tests, the agency initiates a licensing hearing and the board promises reforms without documentation.",
        "reading": "Distinguish regulatory inquiry, compulsory subpoena, inspection and adjudication; each requires its own source of authority and response pathway. Identify the exact regulated entity, statute and jurisdiction before discussing deadlines. Evaluate the evidentiary quality of testing records, investigation interviews and whistleblower chronology. Separate an alleged reporting violation from retaliation and from director oversight questions. Model the hearing's notice, burden, procedural rights, evidentiary rules and remedies under the correct administrative scheme. Draft verifiable corrective actions with owners, dates and controls instead of an assurance of complete compliance. Address limits on privilege, document retention, public comments and truthful agency submissions, and record which specialist legal questions remain open.\n\nEXAMINATION METHOD: Treat the written answer as a legal research and practice portfolio, not an automatically scored quiz. Define the legal question, exact forum, jurisdiction and operative date. Build an annotated chronology and a fact-to-element matrix, keeping facts supported by the record separate from disputed allegations and unknowns. Verify all primary authorities, actual holdings, current rules and any subsequent treatment; do not invent evidence to fill gaps. Address the strongest adverse legal theory, analyze two significant fact changes, and provide a realistic client or court-facing recommendation. The five rubric dimensions total 100 points. A qualified human examiner must evaluate the substantive written submission and its citations before any mastery determination.\n\nTARGETED SOURCE REVIEW (2026-11-03): For federal agencies, 5 U.S.C. §553 addresses covered notice-and-comment rulemaking while §706 defines grounds of judicial review. Neither automatically sets all licensing-hearing evidence rules, inspection powers or state-agency procedures. The final must identify the actual agency, enabling statute, federal or state forum, and procedural posture before assigning burdens or remedies. Sources: https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/553 ; https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/706. Scope: limited identified primary-law or agency-reference check; not a full case citator, state-law verification, individualized legal opinion, or academic sign-off.",
