        "scenario": "Two business partners seek help after a dispute about company finances; one partner separately emails the lawyer confidential allegations against the other.",
        "reading": "Do not begin with a promise to represent both partners. Identify the possible clients: each individual, the company, and any existing clients of the law firm. Conduct a conflict check consistent with the jurisdiction's adopted rules before receiving unnecessary confidential detail. Explain the limits of preliminary consultation, the dangers of common representation when interests diverge, the potential ownership of organizational privilege and the possibility that separate counsel may be required. Use open-ended client interview questions to determine each party's objectives, disputed transactions, immediate deadlines and records without assuming either is truthful or dishonest. Organize options into negotiation, mediation, litigation and governance remedies, identifying decision-makers and procedural consequences. During settlement counseling, distinguish the lawyer's duty to provide accurate risk information from the client's authority to accept or reject a proposal. Ask what information may be shared with a mediator or the other side and whether mediation statements are protected by applicable law. Write a client letter that is clear and noncoercive, acknowledging uncertainty and what additional verification is necessary. This is instructional draft content; verify current controlling legal authority and jurisdiction before relying on it.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): ABA Model Rule 1.7(a) describes concurrent conflicts including directly adverse representation or significant risk of material limitation. Under Model Rule 1.7(b), a client’s written consent is insufficient unless additional conditions such as reasonable competence, legality, and the rule's prohibition on opposing clients in the same tribunal proceeding are satisfied. This model rule is not automatically the adopted rule in every state.\nReviewed text: https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/rule_1_7_conflict_of_interest_current_clients.ssologout/. This is a bounded check of specified provisions, not an exhaustive case-law, citator, later-treatment or state-adoption review.",
