        "reading": "Define the organizational client, authorized contact, service scope, exclusions, timeline and communication expectations. Limited scope must comply with the jurisdiction's professional rules and be reasonable under the circumstances. Distinguish advance deposits from earned fees under applicable trust-account rules; avoid assuming one model fee clause is valid everywhere. Document how a new lawsuit would be screened for conflicts, accepted or referred, and tracked for deadlines. A lawyer should not permit a misunderstood engagement to create missed obligations, and the client should understand who has authority to expand scope.\n\nPRACTICAL REASONING WORKFLOW: Identify the exact governing forum, time period and parties before applying any rule. Prepare a factual chronology and separate facts supported by documents or testimony from allegations, inferences and information not yet available. State the rule in your own words and verify its current primary-law text; a textbook summary is not itself binding authority. Apply the competing arguments above to each necessary legal element and explain what evidence would change the conclusion. In the proposed ruling or advice, address procedure, remedy, costs of uncertainty and professional responsibilities. For self-assessment, compare your first conclusion with an alternative in which one pivotal fact changes and explain precisely which element changes. Retain the written product as a portfolio draft for qualified examiner review.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): ABA Model Rule 1.2(a) reserves settlement decisions to the client, and Rule 1.2(c) permits limited representation only when reasonable under the circumstances and the client gives informed consent. A nonprofit's corporate director is not automatically the lawyer's individual client; document the entity client and authorized representative separately. Apply the jurisdiction's adopted rule rather than assuming the ABA model controls.\nReviewed text: https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/rule_1_2_scope_of_representation_allocation_of_authority_between_client_lawyer/. This is a bounded check of specified provisions, not an exhaustive case-law, citator, later-treatment or state-adoption review.",
