        "scenario": "A firm deposits a client's advance fee into its operating account and later disputes whether it was earned after representation ends.",
        "reading": "Identify the jurisdiction and the nature of the payment before deciding how funds must be handled. Advance fees, true retainers, flat fees and client costs may have different treatments under state rules and engagement language. Compare what was promised, work actually performed, invoices, accounting entries, client authorization and disputed balances. Client property generally demands recordkeeping and segregation where required, while disputed funds may need to remain protected pending resolution under the governing rules. Do not assume every advance fee is earned upon receipt or that all states use identical trust-account mechanics. Prepare a ledger that ties each deposit, fee transfer and refund to supporting records; do not invent amounts where documentation is absent. Explain client communication, complaint pathways and the importance of independent review of potential misconduct. Draft preventive controls so accounting staff cannot treat a lawyer's unsupported instruction as sufficient authority.\n\nAPPLIED PRACTICE AND RESEARCH: Establish the controlling jurisdiction, procedural setting and date before making a legal claim. Create an evidence-to-element matrix distinguishing established facts, allegations, missing records and competing inferences. Cite the exact language of the relevant rule, statute or court decision rather than relying on a secondary summary. Assess how a different material fact would change the likely remedy or professional recommendation. Draft both sides fairly and record unresolved primary-source and later-treatment questions for review by a qualified legal examiner. This is a fictional educational scenario and does not establish personal legal advice or independent competency.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): Under ABA Model Rule 1.15(a), client property must be maintained separately and safeguarded. Model Rule 1.15(c) calls for advance legal fees and expenses to be deposited in a client trust account until earned or incurred, and subsection (e) addresses disputed funds. State fee-classification exceptions and trust-account mechanics vary; do not represent the ABA model as the state's operative statute or rule.\nReviewed text: https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/rule_1_15_safekeeping_property/. This is a bounded check of specified provisions, not an exhaustive case-law, citator, later-treatment or state-adoption review.",
