        "scenario": "After resolving an employment dispute, counsel drafts a broad release that may waive claims not identified in the negotiations.",
        "reading": "Begin with the parties, legal capacity, claims asserted, valuable consideration and exact agreement date. A release should address identified claims and applicable limitations, not assume every conceivable right may be waived. Certain statutory claims and age-discrimination releases have particular requirements; other administrative enforcement rights may not be privately waived. Separate confidentiality, nondisparagement, cooperation, payment timing, breach cure, tax allocation and enforcement jurisdiction. Consider whether a broad provision chills lawful agency reporting or protected communication. Draft the strongest client-side and opposing-side positions regarding future or unknown claims, and check controlling law before using a form clause. A settlement must have a workable method for performance and dispute resolution, including how it interacts with an already pending court case. Identify any term that the client must understand in plain language before deciding whether to sign.\n\nAPPLIED PRACTICE AND RESEARCH: Establish the controlling jurisdiction, procedural setting and date before making a legal claim. Create an evidence-to-element matrix distinguishing established facts, allegations, missing records and competing inferences. Cite the exact language of the relevant rule, statute or court decision rather than relying on a secondary summary. Assess how a different material fact would change the likely remedy or professional recommendation. Draft both sides fairly and record unresolved primary-source and later-treatment questions for review by a qualified legal examiner. This is a fictional educational scenario and does not establish personal legal advice or independent competency.\n\nTARGETED SOURCE REVIEW (2026-11-03): 29 U.S.C. §626(f)(1) specifies statutory requirements for knowing and voluntary waivers of Age Discrimination in Employment Act claims, including written advice to consult an attorney, specified consideration periods and at least seven days to revoke. The 21-day individual and 45-day covered group periods are not interchangeable. A general release cannot waive future ADEA claims and should not be assumed to waive lawful agency participation rights. Sources: https://uscode.house.gov/us/usc/t29/s626/. Scope: limited identified primary-law or agency-reference check; not a full case citator, state-law verification, individualized legal opinion, or academic sign-off.",
