        "scenario": "Two separately represented companies consider sharing internal legal assessments during negotiations over a potential joint defense, but no agreement defines the exchange.",
        "reading": "Distinguish privileged legal advice from underlying business facts and from attorney work product prepared for litigation. Sharing information with another company can create waiver questions; a claim of shared commercial incentives is not necessarily enough for common-interest protection under governing jurisdictional law. Identify who holds each privilege, the purpose and timing of the exchange, the legal interests purportedly aligned and the entities authorized to disclose. A written confidentiality or common-interest agreement may help define expectations but does not magically create a privilege that controlling law does not recognize. Evaluate whether the exchange serves a joint legal strategy or ordinary deal negotiations. Draft a data-sharing protocol with document categories, secure access, limits on forwarding and plans if interests diverge. Explain potential conflict and antitrust risks from sharing competitively sensitive business data. Counsel should also clarify what can be disclosed to independent directors, regulators or a court and how the client can make informed decisions.\n\nPRACTICE METHOD — This is an educational hypothetical, not advice for a real client. Create a chronology and an evidence matrix before reaching a conclusion. Identify legal elements and burdens, jurisdiction, operative date, unresolved facts and source hierarchy. Research any changed statute or precedent and record the date checked. Distinguish what a party alleged from what testimony or documents actually establish. Present a reasonable adverse argument and identify how at least one different fact would affect the legal conclusion or remedy. Prepare your written deliverable for a qualified human examiner rather than treating a successful quiz response as mastery.\n\nTARGETED SOURCE REVIEW (2026-11-03): Upjohn v. United States protects qualifying corporate lawyer-client communications but does not make all underlying business facts privileged. A common-interest arrangement ordinarily depends on valid underlying protection and a sufficiently aligned legal interest under applicable law; merely labelling a commercial confidentiality contract a common-interest agreement cannot create a new privilege. Sources: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/449/383. Scope: limited identified primary-law or agency-reference check; not a full case citator, state-law verification, individualized legal opinion, or academic sign-off.",
