        "scenario": "In settlement talks, one attorney knows that a client witness's prior statement was materially inaccurate, while the client instructs counsel to say the evidence is undisputed.",
        "reading": "Client loyalty does not authorize a lawyer to make knowingly false material factual statements. Separate permissible negotiation positions and value estimates from assertions about presently known facts. Identify exactly what the attorney knows about the prior statement, whether the evidence concerns a material fact, and whether correction is legally required or permitted within the client's confidentiality obligations. Do not assume the lawyer may reveal all confidential information; evaluate applicable ethics exceptions and the selected jurisdiction's adopted rules. Explain to the client the risks of a false representation, available truthful framing, and the need to withdraw from misconduct when required. Determine who has settlement authority and distinguish legal advice from the client's choice to accept an offer. Draft a negotiation script that accurately presents uncertainty and does not hide behind an invented evidentiary certainty. Build a decision tree for changing circumstances, including discovery of a merely mistaken memory versus knowledge of deliberate fabrication.\n\nPRACTICE METHOD — This is an educational hypothetical, not advice for a real client. Create a chronology and an evidence matrix before reaching a conclusion. Identify legal elements and burdens, jurisdiction, operative date, unresolved facts and source hierarchy. Research any changed statute or precedent and record the date checked. Distinguish what a party alleged from what testimony or documents actually establish. Present a reasonable adverse argument and identify how at least one different fact would affect the legal conclusion or remedy. Prepare your written deliverable for a qualified human examiner rather than treating a successful quiz response as mastery.\n\nTARGETED LEGAL-AUTHORITY CROSSCHECK (2026-11-03): ABA Model Rule 4.1 prohibits a lawyer from knowingly making a false statement of material fact or law to a third person; subsection (b) addresses nondisclosure necessary to avoid assisting a client's crime or fraud, subject to Rule 1.6. The ABA's model is educational, not automatically the controlling rule: identify the relevant state's adopted rule and exceptions before opining on disclosure or withdrawal. Primary text/reference: https://www.americanbar.org/content/aba-cms-dotorg/en/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/rule_4_1_truthfulness_in_statements_to_others/. This checks the specified proposition only; subsequent treatment, local law and application to a real record still require further review.",
