        "scenario": "After a safety investigation, executives consider public statements that describe defects as isolated, while an internal test suggests the problem may affect several product lines.",
        "reading": "A corporate crisis response begins by distinguishing verified facts, preliminary findings, legal reporting triggers and reasonable areas of uncertainty. Determine whether the business is public or private, what disclosures are actually required and which persons control statements. Do not automatically apply securities reporting rules to a privately held company, nor treat uncertainty as permission for a known false assertion. Coordinate a legal investigation, product safety evaluation, insurance-notice inquiry and board reporting plan while preserving source records. Build a cross-functional timeline that traces when executives learned of the second test and what had already been communicated to regulators, customers and investors. Analyze how a broader defect could alter mitigation duties, contract claims, shareholder issues or settlement decisions. Draft statements that avoid unsupported assurances and clearly explain what additional tests are pending. Discuss confidentiality and privilege without assuming every internal email becomes protected by involving a lawyer. Provide two decision paths if the preliminary signal is disproved or confirmed.\n\nPRACTICE METHOD — This is an educational hypothetical, not advice for a real client. Create a chronology and an evidence matrix before reaching a conclusion. Identify legal elements and burdens, jurisdiction, operative date, unresolved facts and source hierarchy. Research any changed statute or precedent and record the date checked. Distinguish what a party alleged from what testimony or documents actually establish. Present a reasonable adverse argument and identify how at least one different fact would affect the legal conclusion or remedy. Prepare your written deliverable for a qualified human examiner rather than treating a successful quiz response as mastery.\n\nTARGETED SOURCE REVIEW (2026-11-03): The FTC's corporate data-breach response guide urges accurate public communication, incident scoping and jurisdiction-specific notice assessment. It is not an automatic securities filing obligation: SEC reporting applicability, public company status, materiality and current securities rules must be researched independently. Board and insurer notices also arise from different sources. Sources: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/data-breach-response-guide-business. Scope: limited identified primary-law or agency-reference check; not a full case citator, state-law verification, individualized legal opinion, or academic sign-off.",
